## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Countable Choice, $D=B(x_0,1/4)$, $D'=B(x_0,1/8)$, $m=1/2$, $M=3/2$, $R=1$, and $\gamma=1+\tfrac12\chi_{B(x_0,1/16)}$.

[F1] In dimension two, $X_2(R)$ consists of measurable coefficients equal to $1$ almost everywhere on $D\setminus D'$, satisfying $m\le\gamma\le M$, and with $\max\{\|\gamma\|_{L^1(D)},TV(\gamma;D)\}\le R$. ([[def-ip-calderon-admissible-conductivity-class]], [[def-ip-spatial-bv-and-distributional-tv]])

[F2] For every disk $B(x_0,r)$, its indicator has distributional derivative $-\nu\mathcal H^1|_{\partial B(x_0,r)}$ and total variation $2\pi r$. ([[lem-ip-calderon-disk-inclusion-perimeter-scaling]])

[A1] Countable Choice is assumed in the cited disk-perimeter scaling proof. ([[def-countable-choice]])

## Verification

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 The disk $B(x_0,1/16)$ is contained in $D'$ because $1/16<1/8$, and $\gamma=1$ on $D\setminus D'$. Almost everywhere its only values are $1$ and $3/2$, so $1/2\le\gamma\le3/2$. Thus the support and ellipticity conditions in [F1] hold. [F1, given, algebra]

1.2 Since $|D|=\pi/16$ and $|B(x_0,1/16)|=\pi/256$, the integral satisfies $\|\gamma\|_{L^1(D)}=\pi/16+\pi/512=33\pi/512<1$. By [F2] and homogeneity of distributional variation, $TV(\gamma;D)\le\tfrac12(2\pi/16)=\pi/16<1$; restriction to $D$ cannot increase the full-space variation. Therefore the BV norm is at most $1$, so [F1] gives $\gamma\in X_2(1)$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 At every point of the circle $\partial B(x_0,1/16)$ lying inside $D$, every neighborhood meets the interior disk and its exterior in sets of positive measure. The function equals $3/2$ almost everywhere on the former and $1$ almost everywhere on the latter. A continuous representative would be constant $3/2$ on the interior and constant $1$ on the exterior by continuity and almost-everywhere equality, contradicting continuity across the circle. Hence the conductivity class is discontinuous. [given, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
